+++
title: Assur
description: Former Assyrian capital, now archaeological site in Iraq
hook: People lived at Assur for approximately 3,000 years, from the Early
  Dynastic Period until the Sasanian Empire sacked it in the mid-3rd century
  AD. In 2003 it was added to the World Heritage list of sites in danger,
  because a proposed dam would flood some of it.
standfirst: Aššur, also known as Ashur and Qal'at Sherqat, stood on the
  western bank of the Tigris in what is now Iraq, 100 km (60 mi) south of
  Nineveh. It was the capital of the Middle Assyrian Empire for a time, of the
  Neo-Assyrian Empire (911–609 BC), and a semi-independent state during the
  Parthian Empire.
key-facts:
  - name: Where it is
    value: Saladin Governorate, Iraq, in Mesopotamia
    from: |2-
        location: Saladin Governorate, Iraq
        region: Mesopotamia
  - name: How long it was in use
    value: Early Bronze Age to classical antiquity
    from: "  periods: Early Bronze Age to classical antiquity"
  - name: Built
    value: In the Early Dynastic Period
    from: "    built: Early Dynastic Period"
  - name: Abandoned
    value: 3rd century AD, fully abandoned 14th century AD
    from: "    abandoned: 3rd century AD, fully abandoned 14th century AD"
  - name: World Heritage listing
    value: Listed as Ashur (Qal'at Sherqat), a cultural site under criteria iii
      and iv, designated 2003 (27th session), and endangered 2003–present
