          archaeologists: Friedrich Delitzsch, Robert Koldewey, Walter Andrae, B. Hrouda, R. Dittmann, Peter A. Miglus
pull-quotes:
  - text: '"The andurārum (debt relief) of the Akkadians and their children I
      established. I purified their copper. I established their andurārum from
      the border of the marshes(?) and Ur and [[ Nippur ]], [[ Awal ]], and
      Kismar, [[ Der | der-sumer ]] of the god [[ Ishtaran ]], as far as
      Assur."'
    attribution: Ilusumma, in a text of his, BM 115690, found in the Istar temple at Assur
    section: old-assyrian-period
  - text: '"Dāduša, son of Ipiq-Adad, king of Eshnunna, to Inibšina, his daughter,
      he presented [this weight]."'
    attribution: Inscription on a weight in the shape of a duck found at Assur
    section: isin-larsa-period
timeline:
  - when: 2896–2702 BC
    what: Radiocarbon date of a charcoal sample from the earliest point below the
      foundation of the Ishtar temple.
    from: They retrieved one charcoal sample at the earliest point which was found
      to have a [[ radiocarbon date | radiocarbon-dating ]] of 2896–2702 BC
      (calibration method used [[ IntCal20 | intcal ]]).
  - when: 2600–2350 BC
    what: The Early Dynastic III period, to which later work revised the founding
      of the Ishtar temple, after the early excavators had put it early in the
      3rd millennium BC.
    from: The early excavators had dated the founding of the Ishtar temple to
      early in the 3rd millennium BC but later work had revised that to the
      Early Dynastic III period (2600–2350 BC).[[ ^32 ]]
  - when: 2500 BC
    what: Inception of Assur, according to its Wikidata record.
    from: "inception: 2500 BC"
  - when: c. 2112–2004 BC
    what: Assur is under the control of Ur during the Third Dynasty of Ur.
    from: During the [[ Third Dynasty of Ur ]] (c. 2112–2004 BC), Assur was under
      the control of [[ Ur ]].
  - when: End of the 21st century BC
    what: The Ur III Empire collapses at the hands of the Elamites and control of
      Assur shifts briefly to Eshnunna.
    from: Around the end of the 21st century BC, the [[ Ur III Empire ]] collapsed
      at the hands of the [[ Elamites | elam ]], and the control of Assur
      shifted briefly to [[ Eshnunna ]].
  - when: 1808–1775 BC
    what: Shamshi-Adad I, Amorite ruler of the Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia,
      conquers Assur and makes it his religious capital.
    from: "[[ Shamshi-Adad I ]]'s (1808–1775 BC), [[ Amorite | amorites ]] ruler
      of the [[ Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia ]], eventually conquered Assur
      and made it his religious capital (his primary capital being [[
      Ekallatum ]] and later [[ Shubat-Enlil ]])."
  - when: around 1756 BC
    what: Hammurabi of Babylon incorporates the city into the First Babylonian
      dynasty empire, following the death of Ishme-Dagan I.
    from: However, this empire met its end when [[ Hammurabi ]], the Amorite king
      of [[ Babylon ]] conquered and incorporated the city into the [[ First
      Babylonian dynasty ]] empire following the death of [[ Ishme-Dagan I ]]
      around 1756 BC, while the next three Assyrian kings were viewed as
      vassals of Babylon.[[ ^46 ]]
  - when: 1365 BC to 1050 BC
    what: Assur is once more the throne of an empire, its old temples and palaces
      restored.
    from: The following centuries witnessed the restoration of the old temples and
      palaces of Assur, and the city once more became the throne of an empire
      from 1365 BC to 1050 BC.
  - when: 1244–1208 BC
    what: Tukulti-Ninurta I builds a new temple to the goddess Ishtar on the site
      of the original.
    from: "[[ Tukulti-Ninurta I ]] (1244–1208 BC) also constructed a new temple to
      the goddess Ishtar in the location of the original temple."
  - when: 1115–1075 BC
    what: The Anu-Adad temple is established in the reign of Tiglath-Pileser I.
    from: The [[ Anu ]]-[[ Adad ]] temple was established later during the reign
      of [[ Tiglath-Pileser I ]] (1115–1075 BC).
  - when: 884–859 BC
    what: Ashurnasirpal II moves the capital from Assur to Kalhu.
    from: "[[ Ashurnasirpal II ]] (884–859 BC) moved the capital from Assur to [[
      Kalhu ]] ([[ Calah ]]/[[ Nimrud ]])."
  - when: 705–682 BC
    what: Sennacherib abandons Dur-Sharrukin for Nineveh; at Assur the House of
      the New Year, Akitu, is built and the festivities celebrated.
    from: In the reign of [[ Sennacherib ]] (705–682 BC), the House of the New
      Year, //Akitu//, was built, and the festivities celebrated in the city.
  - when: 549 BC to 330 BC
    what: Assyria is ruled by the Persian Achaemenid Empire, as Athura.
    from: Assyria was ruled by the Persian [[ Achaemenid Empire ]] (as [[ Athura
      ]]) from 549 BC to 330 BC (see [[ Achaemenid Assyria ]]).
  - when: 520 BC
    what: A revolt by the Assyrians takes place, and ultimately fails.
    from: A revolt by the Assyrians took place in 520 BC but ultimately failed.
  - when: 150 BC and 257 AD
    what: The city revives under the Parthians, its population expanding as it
      becomes an administrative centre of Assuristan.
    from: The city revived during the [[ Parthian Empire ]] period, particularly
      between 150 BC and 257 AD, its population expanding and it becoming an
      administrative centre of Parthian-ruled [[ Assuristan ]].
  - when: AD 116
    what: Trajan forms the new Roman provinces of Mesopotamia and Assyria from his
      conquests east of the Euphrates, according to the Roman historian Festus
      writing in about 370.
    from: The Roman historian [[ Festus | festus-historian ]] wrote in about 370
      that in AD 116 [[ Trajan ]] formed from his conquests east of the [[
      Euphrates ]] the new Roman provinces of Mesopotamia and [[ Assyria |
      assyria-roman-province ]].
  - when: c. 257 AD
    what: Ardashir I of the Sasanian Empire captures and sacks Assur, and much of
      its population is dispersed.
    from: Assur was captured and sacked by [[ Ardashir I ]] of the [[ Sasanian
      Empire ]] c. 257 AD, whereafter the city was largely destroyed and much
      (though not all) of its population was dispersed
  - when: 14th century AD
    what: Massacres of Assyrian Christians by Tamurlane; the remaining population
      spreads into the countryside.
    from: with a more sparse Assyrian population enduring until the massacres of
      [[ Assyrian Christians ]] by [[ Tamurlane ]] in the 14th century AD
      after which the remaining population spread into the countryside and the
      area saw an influx of [[ Arabs ]] and [[ Kurds ]].
  - when: "2003"
    what: The site is put on UNESCO's List of World Heritage in Danger, threatened
      by a looming large-scale dam project.
    from: The site was put on [[ UNESCO ]]'s [[ List of World Heritage in Danger
      ]] in 2003, at which time the site was threatened by a looming
      large-scale dam project that would have submerged the ancient
      archaeological site.[[ ^53 ]][[ ^54 ]]
  - when: May 2015
    what: According to some sources, the citadel of Assur is destroyed or badly
      damaged by members of IS using improvised explosive devices.
    from: According to some sources, the citadel of Assur was destroyed or badly
      damaged in May 2015 by members of IS using [[ improvised explosive
      devices | improvised-explosive-device ]].[[ ^56 ]]
  - when: December 2016
    what: An AP report, after Iraqi forces retook the area, says the militants
      tried to destroy the grand entrance arches but they remained standing, a
      local historian calling the damage "minor".
    from: An [[ AP | associated-press ]] report from December 2016 after the Iraqi
      forces had retaken the area, said that the militants tried to destroy
      the city's grand entrance arches, but they remained standing and a local
      historian described the damage as "minor".[[ ^57 ]]
  - when: "2023"
    what: Excavation resumes, by the Assur Excavation Project led by Professor
      Karen Radner with Iraqi and European colleagues.
    from: Excavation at the site resumed in 2023 by the Assur Excavation Project
      led by Professor [[ Karen Radner ]] and a team of Iraqi and European
      colleagues. Work continued in 2024.[[ ^28 ]][[ ^29 ]][[ ^30 ]]
