  - file: Assur_temple.jpg
    role: inline
    section: parthian-empire
    caption: A Parthian temple in Assur. In this period the old temple dedicated
      to the god Ashur was rebuilt, as were temples to other Assyrian gods.
  - file: Flag_of_Iraq.svg
    role: drop
    section: threats-to-assur
    caption: Flag of Iraq.
glossary:
  - term: karum
    gloss: The trading network Assur was a central hub of in the Old Assyrian
      period, stretching through Mesopotamia, Syria, and Anatolia.
    from: In this period Assur was a central hub the "[[ karum | karum-trade-post
      ]]" trading network which stretched through Mesopotamia, Syria, and
      Anatolia.
  - term: limum
    gloss: A city official at Assur appointed yearly to head city finances;
      transactions were dated by their names.
    from: A limum was a city official appointed yearly to head city finances.
      Transactions were dated by their names in [[ Limmu ]] (eponyms).
  - term: andurārum
    gloss: Debt relief; Ilusumma's text says he established it for the Akkadians
      and their children.
    from: '"The andurārum (debt relief) of the Akkadians and their children I
      established.'
  - term: stratigraphy
    gloss: The sequence of construction and occupation levels at a site; at Assur,
      later periods left so much construction overburden that a full one was
      only possible at the Ishtar temple.
    from: Because of the large construction overburden from later periods a full
      [[ stratigraphy | stratigraphy-archaeology ]] was only possible at the
      site of the Ishtar temple in the Nabu district.
  - term: Athura
    gloss: The name under which Assyria was ruled by the Persian Achaemenid Empire
      from 549 BC to 330 BC.
    from: Assyria was ruled by the Persian [[ Achaemenid Empire ]] (as [[ Athura
      ]]) from 549 BC to 330 BC (see [[ Achaemenid Assyria ]]).
  - term: Assuristan
    gloss: Parthian-ruled Assyria, of which Assur became an administrative centre.
    from: its population expanding and it becoming an administrative centre of
      Parthian-ruled [[ Assuristan ]]
  - term: Akitu
    gloss: The House of the New Year, built at Assur in the reign of Sennacherib,
      where the festivities were celebrated.
    from: In the reign of [[ Sennacherib ]] (705–682 BC), the House of the New
      Year, //Akitu//, was built, and the festivities celebrated in the city.
  - term: IntCal20
    gloss: The calibration method used for the radiocarbon date of 2896–2702 BC
      from the charcoal sample below the Ishtar temple.
    from: which was found to have a [[ radiocarbon date | radiocarbon-dating ]] of
      2896–2702 BC (calibration method used [[ IntCal20 | intcal ]])
further:
  - slug: neo-assyrian-empire
    why: Assur was its capital until Ashurnasirpal II moved to Kalhu, and it
      stayed the empire's religious centre because of the temple of the
      national god Ashur.
  - slug: old-assyrian-period
    why: The period when Assur was a central hub of the karum trading network
      through Mesopotamia, Syria and Anatolia, ruled by a city assembly.
  - slug: nimrud
    why: Kalhu, the biblical Calah, 65 kilometres (40 mi) north of Assur, and the
      capital Ashurnasirpal II moved to.
anchors:
  hook: Occupation of the city itself continued for approximately 3,000 years,[[
    ^2 ]] from the [[ Early Dynastic Period |
    early-dynastic-period-mesopotamia ]] to the mid-3rd century AD, when the
    city was sacked by the [[ Sasanian Empire ]], after which it was sparsely
    populated until the massacres of [[ Assyrian Christians ]] conducted by [[
    Tamurlane ]] in the 14th century AD after which the remaining population
    relocated to the countryside. The site is a [[ World Heritage Site ]] and
    was added to that organization's list of [[ sites in danger |
    list-of-world-heritage-in-danger ]] in 2003 as a result of a proposed dam,
    which would flood some of the site.
  standfirst: "!!Aššur!!,[[ ^a ]] also known as !!Ashur!! and !!Qal'at
    Sherqat!!, was the capital of the [[ Middle Assyrian Empire ]] for a time,
    of the [[ Neo-Assyrian Empire ]] (911–609 BC) and a semi-independent state
    during the [[ Parthian Empire ]] between the 2nd century BC and mid 3rd
    century AD. The remains of the city lie on the western bank of the [[
    Tigris River ]], north of the confluence with its tributary, the [[ Little
    Zab ]], in what is now [[ Iraq ]], more precisely in the [[ al-Shirqat
    District ]] of the [[ Saladin Governorate ]]. Assur lies 65 kilometres (40
    mi) south of the site of [[ Kalhu ]] (the biblical [[ Calah ]], [[ Nimrud
    ]]) and 100 km (60 mi) south of [[ Nineveh ]]."
